" -H "AccountKey: $LTA_KEY" "https://datamall2.mytransport.sg/ltaodataservice/v3/BusArrival?BusStopCode=04121" Copy On Windows: PowerShell Press the Windows key, type PowerShell and open Windows PowerShell or Terminal . Type $k = Read-Host "Paste LTA key" and press Enter . Paste your AccountKey with a right-click or Ctrl + V and press Enter . Click Copy on the line below, paste it into PowerShell and press Enter . It says curl.exe , not curl , because in Windows PowerShell curl is a different command that rejects these options. Read the last line. HTTP status: 200 , with JSON above it, means it worked. Select the JSON, copy it with Ctrl + C and paste it into the CONTEXT block of the master prompt. Type Remove-Variable k and press Enter , or close the window. curl.exe -sS -w "
HTTP status: %{http_code}
" -H "AccountKey: $k" "https://datamall2.mytransport.sg/ltaodataservice/v3/BusArrival?BusStopCode=04121" Copy If your screen differs and PowerShell answers with a red error about a parameter, check that the line starts with curl.exe . If the last line is not 200. 401 means the key is wrong, so repeat steps 2 to 4 and paste it again. 404 means a typo in the header name or the address. 403 , with an Access Denied page, means BusStopCode is missing or wrongly capitalised, and it must be spelled exactly that way. Why Terminal and not the browser The address bar cannot send a header, so it always answers 404, and the browser blocks a console fetch from any other site. Terminal is the simplest place to make this call by hand. The key never appears in the command itself, which is why step 2 reads it into a variable first. You do this once, to learn the field names; after that, only your function calls LTA. Building Autonomous AI Agents &middot; Day 1 &middot; Full End-to-End Build Prof. Sungjong Roh, PhD &middot; SMU &middot; Lee Kong Chian School of Business What you are aiming at, by 15:40 SMU &middot; Stamford Rd 04121 &middot; updated 8s ago 7 Arriving seats available &middot; double deck 14 4 min standing available 16 9 min seats available 111 12 min seats available &middot; wheelchair 175 — not running at this hour Contains information from LTA DataMall Bus Arrival, accessed [today's date] from the Land Transport Authority, which is made available under the terms of the Singapore Open Data Licence version 1.0, data.gov.sg/open-data-licence The attribution line, ready to paste Contains information from LTA DataMall Bus Arrival accessed on [DATE] from the Land Transport Authority (LTA DataMall), which is made available under the terms of the Singapore Open Data Licence version 1.0 https://data.gov.sg/open-data-licence. This is an SMU course project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Land Transport Authority. Copy Replace [DATE] with the day you called LTA, or ask the agent to show the current date there. The licence requires a visible source notice with a link to the licence. The last sentence is not politeness either. The licence forbids suggesting official status or LTA endorsement , and a bus app is exactly the kind of thing a passer-by would assume is official unless told otherwise, so choose an app name that is clearly your own. Look at the last row A service with no buses running shows a plain sentence rather than a zero, a blank, or an error. Deciding what your product says when it has nothing to say is design work , not an edge case to handle later, and it is the difference between a demonstration and something a person could actually rely on. Building Autonomous AI Agents &middot; Day 1 &middot; Full End-to-End Build Prof. Sungjong Roh, PhD &middot; SMU &middot; Lee Kong Chian School of Business When it breaks, part one: the failures of wiring What you see What is actually wrong The boundary it teaches 404 from LTA at an address you believe is right The header never arrived, its name is misspelled ( Account-Key ), or the path is mis-cased. Code that sends an empty string for a missing variable also lands here. Plain http is not a cause: LTA redirects it to https . read the address one character at a time before you touch the key 403 with an HTML page saying Access Denied The BusStopCode parameter is missing or wrongly capitalised. It must be spelled BusStopCode exactly. the parameter name is case-sensitive, just like the path 404 from your own /api/bus Vercel never built a function there. The file is inside src/ or misnamed, the Framework Preset is not Vite, or the live deployment is older than your push. check the file tree on GitHub: if api/ is not at the root there, no amount of redeploying will help 401 with an empty body Two faults land here and look identical. Either the value is wrong, usually a trailing space from the email, or the variable was never set, the agent skipped the 503 guard, and your code sent the word undefined , because that is what JavaScript does with a missing value. read keyConfigured from your health endpoint; it is the only thing that separates them The key is readable in the deployed page It was typed into a file, or it was given a name beginning VITE_ . the prefix decides the audience, and VITE_ means everyone Green deployment, blank white page The build succeeded and the code failed while running in the browser. a build log cannot explain a runtime failure, so open the browser console Read the two together A 404 and a 401 look equally like failure and point in different directions. The 404 is about the address and the header; the 401 is about the value inside the header. Neither tells you whether you forgot to redeploy, which is why the health endpoint you built is the first thing to open rather than the last. Building Autonomous AI Agents &middot; Day 1 &middot; Full End-to-End Build Prof. Sungjong Roh, PhD &middot; SMU &middot; Lee Kong Chian School of Business When it breaks, part two: the failures of data and of time What you see What is actually wrong The boundary it teaches The panel is empty, but the call returned 200 You are reading a path that does not exist inside a perfectly good answer. print the real response, because guessed field names are the top cause Only four digits in the stop code The leading zero was dropped, because community sites display the code stripped. an identifier is a string, and never a number The function returns 504 If the error page or the Logs row says FUNCTION_INVOCATION_TIMEOUT , your function ran for five minutes without answering, usually a missing response or a call with no timeout. A 504 without that code is probably LTA's own, passed through by your function. read the error code before you blame LTA: only that name means your own function hung Nothing listed after midnight Nothing is wrong. Services are not running, and LTA answers with an empty list. an empty answer is not an error, and your screen has to say so in words The logs are empty the next morning Runtime logs are kept for one hour on the free plan. Their times are in UTC, eight hours behind Singapore. look while it is broken, rather than after the break Show and tell Every URL goes on the screen at 17:15. Groups whose build broke present the failure : name what you saw, then name the boundary it turned out to be. Those are the most useful two minutes of the day for everybody else in the room. Building Autonomous AI Agents &middot; Day 1 &middot; Full End-to-End Build Prof. Sungjong Roh, PhD &middot; SMU &middot; Lee Kong Chian School of Business What to take away Question The answer this session gives you What was the 10:30 screen missing? Data that is true now, something that computes, and a private place for a secret. The rest of the day added the first and the third; the computing half is the model, and tomorrow's agent reaches it through this identical pattern. What is an API? An address that answers in a format a program can read. Four parts go out: address, parameters, headers, method. Two come back: a status code and a body. Why route through my own function? Two reasons at once. The browser will not read a response from a service that does not permit it, and a key in the browser is a published key. The detour solves both. Where does the key live? In a Vercel environment variable whose Type is Secret, read with process.env inside /api , and in AI Studio's Secrets panel for the preview. Never in the code, never in the repository, never in a prompt, never behind a VITE_ name. What does LTA do when it is unhappy? 404 when the header never arrived or the address is wrong, 403 when BusStopCode is missing or mis-cased, 401 when some value arrived and was refused. A missing variable lands on 401 too when the code has no 503 guard, because JavaScript sends the word undefined rather than nothing, so your own health endpoint is what tells a wrong key from a missing one. How do I know it is working? A health endpoint you built, checked before the app; then the Logs tab within the hour; then the status pages, remembering that they do not watch bus arrivals. When is it a product? When two sources meet and answer a question neither could alone, and when the screen still says something sensible at one in the morning with no buses running. Principle You have now built the shape that every keyed service in this course will use: a credential on the server, a function in the middle, a page that only ever talks to its own address. Tomorrow a model sits in front of those functions and decides which one to call , and that is an agent: Day 2 builds it with LangChain, LangGraph and MCP. Building Autonomous AI Agents &middot; Day 1 &middot; Full End-to-End Build Prof. Sungjong Roh, PhD &middot; SMU &middot; Lee Kong Chian School of Business Tonight, before Day 2 What to do tonight 1 Keep the page alive and change one thing on it. Add a second stop, or make it say something friendlier when nothing is running, then push from AI Studio and watch Vercel deploy it. The point is that the loop from prompt to push to live URL stays warm in your hands. If LTA stops answering, look for a maintenance banner on the datamall.lta.gov.sg home page, which is where LTA announces planned outages. 2 Bring the URL and the key. Note the production address listed under Domains ( your-project.vercel.app ) and check that /api/health still says keyConfigured: true . If your group ran on the driver's key today, request your own tonight: LTA's terms ask you to keep a key secure and confidential, so do not share it, even with teammates. 3 Come back at 09:00 for Day 2. You build agents with LangChain, LangGraph and MCP , and the /api functions you deployed today become the tools those agents call. Check tonight that /api/bus still answers, because tomorrow's agent calls it. Why the product came before the agents An agent is a model that decides which tool to call and what to pass it. Every one of those tools is a function like the one you deployed today: a key on the server, a request to a service, and an answer returned in a format a program can read. You have now built that end to end once , with an agent writing the code and you checking what it wrote. Tomorrow's frameworks assume you already know what a key is, where it lives, what a function does, and how to tell whether something is working. You learned all four today, on data you could check by walking downstairs. One last thing Walk past stop 04121 on your way out and look at the display. Your screen and that screen are now reading the same feed , which was not true of the screen you shipped at 10:30. Building Autonomous AI Agents &middot; Day 1 &middot; Full End-to-End Build Prof. Sungjong Roh, PhD &middot; SMU &middot; Lee Kong Chian School of Business Thank You for Being Curious Prof. Sungjong Roh, PhD Building Autonomous AI Agents &middot; Day 2 tomorrow at 09:00 &middot; TalkToRoh.com Academic Director, MBAI &middot; Lee Kong Chian School of Business &middot; SMU &middot; Day 1 &middot; September 2026 Cohort Press ? for shortcuts · F step-reveal · A all-at-once · S presenter &times; Navigation & shortcuts &rarr; Space Reveal next point &middot; then next slide &larr; Hide last point &middot; then previous slide Home End First / last slide 1 – 9 Jump to section F Fullscreen &middot; content revealed point-by-point A Fullscreen &middot; everything shown, move slide-by-slide S Presenter view (current + next + timer) Esc Close / exit mode Building Autonomous AI Agents · Day 1 · Full End-to-End Build · SMU
